' Legend
legend right
  <b>Mockito Spy Concepts:</b>

  <b>What is a Spy?</b>
  A spy is a wrapper around a REAL object
  that delegates to actual method implementations
  by default, but allows selective stubbing of
  specific methods.

  <b>Key Characteristics:</b>
  • Wraps real object (not empty shell)
  • Calls real methods by default
  • Allows selective method stubbing
  • Shares state with real object
  • Supports verification like mocks

  <b>Creating Spies:</b>
  1. spy(realObject)
  2. @Spy annotation on field
  3. spy(new Class())

  <b>Stubbing Spies - IMPORTANT:</b>
  ✓ Use: doReturn(value).when(spy).method()
  ✗ Avoid: when(spy.method()).thenReturn(value)

  Reason: when() calls real method first,
  can trigger unwanted side effects!

  <b>When to Use Spies:</b>
  ✓ Legacy code with complex dependencies
  ✓ Partial mocking (mostly real, some stubbed)
  ✓ Verifying real object behavior
  ✓ Testing abstract class concrete methods
  ✓ Semi-integration tests

  <b>When NOT to Use Spies:</b>
  ✗ Pure unit tests (use mocks)
  ✗ Methods with side effects you want to avoid
  ✗ Final classes (requires special config)
  ✗ Simple objects without dependencies

  <b>Spy Limitations:</b>
  • Cannot spy on final classes (default)
  • Cannot spy on null objects
  • Cannot spy on primitives
  • Cannot spy on interfaces (need implementation)
  • Side effects are REAL

  <b>Real vs Stubbed Methods:</b>
  Real method: Executes actual implementation
  Stubbed method: Returns stubbed value

  <b>State Sharing:</b>
  Spy and real object share the SAME state.
  Changes through spy affect real object!

  <b>Example:</b>
  User real = new User("jdoe", "john@test.com");
  User spy = spy(real);

  // Real method called
  spy.getEmail();  // returns "john@test.com"

  // Stub specific method
  doReturn("fake@test.com").when(spy).getEmail();
  spy.getEmail();  // returns "fake@test.com"

  // Other methods still real
  spy.getUsername();  // returns "jdoe"

  <b>Best Practices:</b>
  1. Use doReturn() instead of when() for spies
  2. Be aware of state sharing
  3. Use spies sparingly (design smell?)
  4. Verify both stubbed and real calls
  5. Consider if design could be improved
  6. Prefer mocks for pure unit tests

  <b>Verification:</b>
  verify(spy).method();  // Works for all calls
  verify(spy, times(2)).method();
  verify(spy, never()).method();

  InOrder inOrder = inOrder(spy);
  inOrder.verify(spy).method1();
  inOrder.verify(spy).method2();
endlegend

